<div class="sasha-practice-page">

  <div class="sasha-container">


    <!-- ============================================
         HEADER
    ============================================= -->

    <header class="sasha-practice-page-hero">

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-eyebrow">
        Sasha Best Practice
      </p>

      <h1>
        Pricing Review
      </h1>

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-intro">
        Review pricing before submission to make sure the
        proposed fee is consistent with the scope, delivery
        approach, risks, client expectations, and the
        proposal's overall win strategy.
      </p>

    </header>


    <!-- ============================================
         WHY REVIEW PRICING
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Why conduct a pricing review?
        </h2>

        <p>
          Pricing is more than a final number.
        </p>

        <p>
          It reflects assumptions about scope, staffing,
          effort, risk, schedule, project management,
          subconsultants, expenses, and the way the team
          intends to deliver the work.
        </p>

        <p>
          A pricing review helps the proposal team determine
          whether those assumptions are consistent with the
          technical proposal and whether the resulting fee
          makes sense for the pursuit.
        </p>

        <p>
          The goal is not simply to make the price lower.
          The goal is to make sure it is credible,
          competitive, defensible, and aligned with the
          strategy.
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         START WITH THE RFP
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Start with the RFP
        </h2>

        <p>
          Before reviewing the fee, confirm exactly how the
          client requires pricing to be presented and
          evaluated.
        </p>

        <p>
          Look for:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            required pricing forms or schedules;
          </li>

          <li>
            fixed-fee, upset-limit, hourly, unit-rate,
            or other pricing requirements;
          </li>

          <li>
            instructions for taxes and reimbursable
            expenses;
          </li>

          <li>
            requirements for subconsultant fees;
          </li>

          <li>
            provisional items or optional services;
          </li>

          <li>
            mandatory breakdowns by task, phase, person,
            discipline, or deliverable;
          </li>

          <li>
            prescribed hourly rates;
          </li>

          <li>
            requirements for level of effort;
          </li>

          <li>
            pricing evaluation formulas; and
          </li>

          <li>
            any rules that could cause a pricing submission
            to be rejected as non-compliant.
          </li>

        </ul>

        <p>
          These requirements should govern the structure of
          the pricing review.
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         COMPARE PRICE TO SCOPE
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Compare the price to the proposed scope
        </h2>

        <p>
          Every significant commitment in the technical
          proposal should have enough effort behind it to
          make delivery credible.
        </p>

        <p>
          Review the pricing against:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            the proposed methodology;
          </li>

          <li>
            the work plan;
          </li>

          <li>
            project phases and deliverables;
          </li>

          <li>
            meetings and workshops;
          </li>

          <li>
            consultation requirements;
          </li>

          <li>
            field work and site visits;
          </li>

          <li>
            quality assurance and quality control;
          </li>

          <li>
            project management;
          </li>

          <li>
            specialist and subconsultant involvement;
          </li>

          <li>
            reviews, revisions, and client comments; and
          </li>

          <li>
            final production and submission requirements.
          </li>

        </ul>

        <p>
          If the proposal promises an activity, deliverable,
          review, or level of service that does not appear
          to be supported by the fee, investigate the gap.
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         REVIEW LEVEL OF EFFORT
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Review the level of effort
        </h2>

        <p>
          A pricing review should examine not only the total
          fee, but how the effort is distributed.
        </p>

        <p>
          Ask whether:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            enough senior time has been provided for
            important decisions and client interaction;
          </li>

          <li>
            project management effort is realistic;
          </li>

          <li>
            technical tasks have sufficient production
            hours;
          </li>

          <li>
            quality reviews are properly allowed for;
          </li>

          <li>
            coordination between disciplines has been
            recognized;
          </li>

          <li>
            meetings and consultation activities have been
            fully accounted for;
          </li>

          <li>
            junior and senior resources are being used
            appropriately; and
          </li>

          <li>
            the staffing pattern matches the responsibilities
            described in the proposal.
          </li>

        </ul>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         REVIEW ASSUMPTIONS
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Review the assumptions behind the price
        </h2>

        <p>
          Pricing often depends on assumptions that are not
          obvious from the final fee.
        </p>

        <p>
          Identify assumptions relating to:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            number of meetings;
          </li>

          <li>
            number of design or review cycles;
          </li>

          <li>
            client response times;
          </li>

          <li>
            information to be supplied by the client;
          </li>

          <li>
            site access;
          </li>

          <li>
            quantity of field work;
          </li>

          <li>
            third-party approvals;
          </li>

          <li>
            stakeholder participation;
          </li>

          <li>
            subconsultant scope;
          </li>

          <li>
            schedule duration; and
          </li>

          <li>
            responsibilities that are excluded from the fee.
          </li>

        </ul>

        <p>
          If an assumption is important to the validity of
          the price, the proposal team should understand it
          before submission.
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         REVIEW RISK
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Test the price against the pursuit risks
        </h2>

        <p>
          Review the risks already identified during
          proposal planning and ask whether the price
          appropriately recognizes them.
        </p>

        <p>
          Pay particular attention to:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            uncertain scope;
          </li>

          <li>
            aggressive schedules;
          </li>

          <li>
            difficult approvals;
          </li>

          <li>
            extensive stakeholder coordination;
          </li>

          <li>
            incomplete background information;
          </li>

          <li>
            unusual technical complexity;
          </li>

          <li>
            third-party dependencies; and
          </li>

          <li>
            activities that could require significant
            rework.
          </li>

        </ul>

        <p>
          A price that ignores known risks may appear
          competitive during the pursuit but create serious
          delivery problems after award.
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         REVIEW COMPETITIVENESS
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Consider competitiveness
        </h2>

        <p>
          Once the team is satisfied that the fee can
          support the proposed work, consider how the price
          is likely to perform competitively.
        </p>

        <p>
          Useful questions include:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            How heavily is price weighted in the
            evaluation?
          </li>

          <li>
            Is the client known to favour low price,
            technical quality, or overall value?
          </li>

          <li>
            Are there incumbent or market conditions that
            may affect competitor pricing?
          </li>

          <li>
            Is the proposed approach more expensive because
            it includes services the client values?
          </li>

          <li>
            Are there efficiencies that could reduce cost
            without weakening the proposal?
          </li>

          <li>
            Would a lower fee introduce unacceptable
            delivery risk?
          </li>

        </ul>

        <p>
          The objective is to understand the strategic
          implications of the price rather than reducing it
          automatically.
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         ALIGN PRICE WITH WIN STRATEGY
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Align the price with the win strategy
        </h2>

        <p>
          The price should support the story the proposal
          tells.
        </p>

        <p>
          If the win strategy emphasizes responsiveness,
          senior involvement, risk reduction, accelerated
          delivery, stakeholder engagement, innovation, or
          another service advantage, the fee should contain
          enough effort to deliver that promise.
        </p>

        <p>
          Ask:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            What are we promising the client?
          </li>

          <li>
            Where is the effort required to deliver that
            promise?
          </li>

          <li>
            Is the pricing structure consistent with the
            proposed approach?
          </li>

          <li>
            Are we paying for activities that do not
            strengthen the submission?
          </li>

          <li>
            Are we underpricing anything that is central to
            our strategy?
          </li>

        </ul>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         WHAT SASHA DOES
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          What Sasha can help review
        </h2>

        <p>
          Sasha can help compare the proposed pricing with
          the information already developed during proposal
          planning.
        </p>

        <p>
          She can help identify:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            inconsistencies between the work plan and the
            fee;
          </li>

          <li>
            commitments that appear to be under-resourced;
          </li>

          <li>
            significant activities that may be missing from
            the pricing;
          </li>

          <li>
            staffing patterns that do not match proposed
            responsibilities;
          </li>

          <li>
            assumptions that should be discussed before
            submission;
          </li>

          <li>
            risks that may not be reflected in the fee; and
          </li>

          <li>
            opportunities to align pricing more closely
            with the win strategy.
          </li>

        </ul>

        <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

          <strong>
            Sasha's role
          </strong>

          <span>
            Sasha does not replace the firm's estimator,
            project manager, financial reviewer, or authorized
            pricing decision-maker. Her role is to help the
            proposal team conduct a disciplined review of the
            pricing in the context of the pursuit.
          </span>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         FINAL CHECK
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Conduct a final pricing check
        </h2>

        <p>
          Before submission, confirm that:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            all required pricing forms are complete;
          </li>

          <li>
            totals and subtotals reconcile;
          </li>

          <li>
            hourly rates and quantities are correct;
          </li>

          <li>
            subconsultant costs are included where required;
          </li>

          <li>
            expenses and taxes are handled according to the
            RFP instructions;
          </li>

          <li>
            optional and provisional items are clearly
            identified;
          </li>

          <li>
            the fee matches the final technical scope;
          </li>

          <li>
            no late proposal changes have created an
            unpriced commitment; and
          </li>

          <li>
            the person authorized to approve the fee has
            reviewed the final pricing.
          </li>

        </ul>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         PRACTICAL OUTCOME
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          What you should have when the review is complete
        </h2>

        <p>
          At the end of the pricing review, the proposal
          team should understand:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            what the price includes;
          </li>

          <li>
            the major assumptions behind it;
          </li>

          <li>
            how the effort supports the proposed approach;
          </li>

          <li>
            whether known pursuit risks have been
            considered;
          </li>

          <li>
            whether the pricing is compliant with the RFP;
          </li>

          <li>
            whether the price supports the win strategy;
            and
          </li>

          <li>
            whether any issues require resolution before
            final approval.
          </li>

        </ul>

        <p>
          The result should be a price that the team can
          explain, defend, and deliver — not simply a number
          completed at the end of the proposal process.
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>

{{!-- =================================================
     DECISION
================================================== --}}

<section
  class="sasha-practice-page-section
         sasha-practice-page-decision"
  aria-labelledby="pricing-review-decision"
>

  <div class="sasha-practice-decision-panel">

    <div>

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
        ADD TO THIS PURSUIT?
      </p>

      <h2 id="pricing-review-decision">
        Would you like to use a Pricing Review?
      </h2>

      <p>
        Sasha will use this choice when creating the proposal
        schedule and will include this practice if you select Yes.
      </p>

    </div>


    <form
      method="POST"
      action="/plan/practice-decision"
      class="sasha-practice-decision-form"
    >

      {{#if csrfToken}}

        <input
          type="hidden"
          name="_csrf"
          value="{{csrfToken}}"
        >

      {{/if}}


      <input
        type="hidden"
        name="pursuitId"
        value="{{proposal._id}}"
      >


      <input
        type="hidden"
        name="practiceKey"
        value="pricing-review"
      >


      <fieldset class="sasha-practice-page-options">

        <legend class="visually-hidden">
          Use a Pricing Review
        </legend>


<label>

  <input
    type="radio"
    name="decision"
    value="yes"
    {{#if practiceSelected}}
      checked
    {{/if}}
  >

  <span>
    Yes
  </span>

</label>


<label>

  <input
    type="radio"
    name="decision"
    value="no"
    {{#unless practiceSelected}}
      checked
    {{/unless}}
  >

  <span>
    No
  </span>

</label>

      </fieldset>


      <button
        type="submit"
        class="sasha-plan-card-action"
      >
        Save Choice
      </button>

    </form>

  </div>

</section>
    <!-- ============================================
         RETURN TO PLAN
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <a
          href="/plan{{#if pursuitId}}?pursuit={{pursuitId}}{{/if}}"
        >
          Return to Plan
        </a>

      </div>

    </section>


  </div>

</div>